<p>After a hurricane or tropical storm, the most common argument on the Coastal Bend is not whether a home was damaged. It is what damaged it. <strong>Wind vs. storm surge damage claims</strong> come down to cause, because the cause decides which policy responds. A torn roof and a soaked ceiling usually point to wind. A horizontal water line across the drywall and mud on the floor usually point to surge or rising water. When both show up in the same house, the windstorm insurer and the flood insurer may each argue the other should pay. This article explains why that happens and how the evidence is built, from the perspective of a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster who documents these losses.</p>

<h2>Why wind and water claims split on the coast</h2>

<p>Most coastal homes in Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties sit in the TWIA catastrophe area. TWIA policies cover wind and hail only; TWIA itself says no other perils are covered. Standard homeowners policies generally exclude flood, and flood coverage comes from the National Flood Insurance Program or a private flood policy. That leaves many owners with separate windstorm and flood policies, each with its own adjuster, deductible, and limits. Our <a href="/hurricane-damage-claims-corpus-christi-guide/">Corpus Christi hurricane damage claims guide</a> explains this three-policy setup in more detail.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type of damage</th><th>Typical evidence</th><th>Policy that usually responds</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Roof covering, decking, flashing torn off</td><td>Missing shingles or panels, creased tabs, debris scattered downwind</td><td>Windstorm policy (TWIA or private wind)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain entering through a wind-created opening</td><td>Ceiling stains below the opening, wet insulation, water running down walls</td><td>Often the windstorm policy, depending on policy terms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storm surge entering at ground level</td><td>Horizontal water line, sand and silt, marine debris, damage from the floor up</td><td>Flood policy (NFIP or private)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Structural damage at lower levels from wave action</td><td>Punched-in walls or garage doors, displaced framing, scoured soil</td><td>Usually flood; may be disputed if wind also acted on the structure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debris impact above the water line</td><td>Holes and dents high on walls or roof, tree limbs, airborne objects</td><td>Usually windstorm</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What Coastal Bend surge has looked like</h2>

<p>Surge is not limited to the strongest storms. The NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database documents surge from storms of very different strengths:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Hurricane Harvey, Aug. 2017.</strong> NCEI reports storm tides of 6 to 8 feet in Port Aransas as surge entered from Corpus Christi and Redfish Bays (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=720926" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>). In Aransas County, surge punched holes through walls and garage doors on the lower portions of most homes in Holiday Beach and Copano Village (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=718740" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Hurricane Hanna, July 25, 2020.</strong> Tide levels reached 2 to 4 feet above ground level in coastal Nueces County, damaging parts of the Corpus Christi Marina and numerous piers and inundating North Beach (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=912741" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>). On the Nueces County islands, levels reached 3 to 4.5 feet above ground and damaged Bob Hall Pier (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=912743" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Tropical Storm Harold, Aug. 22, 2023.</strong> Tides reached 2.6 feet above mean higher high water (MHHW) at the Nueces Bay TCOON site and 2.43 feet MHHW at the Viola Turning Basin (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1133268" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>). An NWS survey found 3.30 feet MHHW at Whitecap Beach on north Padre Island (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1133829" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>).</li>
</ul>

<p>Harold also brought wind: NCEI records a 62 mph gust at Corpus Christi International Airport the same day. Wind and water arriving together is exactly what makes these claims hard to sort out. Our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history page</a> and <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> can help you find events for your area.</p>

<h2>Building the evidence: how cause is shown</h2>

<h3>Water lines</h3>
<p>Rising water usually leaves a horizontal line at a consistent height across walls, cabinets, furniture, fences, and exterior siding. Photograph it in every room and outside, with a tape measure in the frame showing height above the floor and above the ground. Water that came from above leaves a different pattern: stains that start at the ceiling, run down walls, and are worst under a roof or window opening.</p>

<h3>Debris and deposits</h3>
<p>Surge tends to leave sand, silt, seaweed, marine grass, and floating debris, often in a line (a wrack line) at the high-water mark. Wind tends to scatter shingles, panels, and limbs, and can leave impact marks above any water line. Photograph debris where it landed before it is moved.</p>

<h3>Damage pattern and direction</h3>
<p>Look at where damage starts. Damage that begins at the roof and works down suggests wind and wind-driven rain. Damage that begins at the floor and works up, with nothing above a certain height, suggests water. Structural damage on the side facing the bay at low levels may point to wave action; damage on the windward side high up points to wind.</p>

<h3>Timing</h3>
<p>When did the roof fail compared with when the water rose? Neighbors’ photos, doorbell cameras, phone videos, and texts with time stamps can all help. Timing is often where tide gauge data becomes useful.</p>

