<h2>Using NOAA tide gauge data</h2>

<p>NOAA’s Center for Operational Oceanographic Products and Services (CO-OPS) runs water level stations around the Coastal Bend, with data at <a href="https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov</a>. Stations near Corpus Christi include:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Station ID</th><th>Name</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>8775792</td><td>Packery Channel</td><td>Water level data available</td></tr>
<tr><td>8775296</td><td>USS Lexington, Corpus Christi Bay</td><td>Water level data available</td></tr>
<tr><td>8775237</td><td>Port Aransas</td><td>Water level data available</td></tr>
<tr><td>8774770</td><td>Rockport</td><td>Water level data available</td></tr>
<tr><td>8775870</td><td>Bob Hall Pier, Corpus Christi</td><td>Tide predictions only when checked in October 2026</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>For a past storm, the station record can show when water began rising, the peak level, and how long it stayed high. Two terms are worth knowing. <strong>Storm surge</strong> is the rise above the predicted tide. <strong>Storm tide</strong> is the total water level, surge plus tide. The NCEI record for Hurricane Nicholas in September 2021 shows the difference: Bob Hall Pier measured a maximum storm tide of 2.07 feet MHHW, and because the surge arrived near high tide, the maximum surge itself was 1.53 feet (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=985053" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>).</p>

<p>Gauge data has limits. It measures water at the station, not at your property, and local ground elevation, shoreline shape, and wave action all change what happens at a specific address. Use it to support your timeline and on-site measurements, not as a substitute. Our article on <a href="/noaa-weather-records-document-property-damage-corpus-christi/">using NOAA weather records to document property damage</a> covers related tools.</p>

<h2>Combined causes: a general overview</h2>

<p>Sometimes wind and water both contribute to the same damage. A roof can be torn open by wind while surge floods the first floor, and the interior damage can be a mix of both. Insurance policies handle this differently. Some contain language that addresses losses caused by a combination of covered and excluded causes, sometimes called anti-concurrent causation wording. How that language applies to a specific loss is a legal question that depends on the policy and on Texas law. A public adjuster can help gather and present the evidence of what caused each part of the damage, but if a claim is denied on combined-cause grounds, talk to a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>Practical tips for wind vs. surge claims</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>File with both insurers</strong> if wind and water both reached the property. Do not wait for one to decide before reporting to the other.</li>
<li><strong>Measure and photograph water lines</strong> inside and outside before cleanup or painting.</li>
<li><strong>Document the roof and upper walls separately</strong> from lower-level damage, so each adjuster sees the damage for their peril.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a timeline</strong> with what you saw, when, and any time-stamped photos or videos.</li>
<li><strong>Compare estimates side by side.</strong> Make sure items are not left out of both, such as drywall above the water line damaged by roof leaks, or counted twice.</li>
<li><strong>Know your elevated-home limits.</strong> Many island homes are elevated. NFIP policies limit coverage for enclosures and items below the lowest elevated floor of many elevated buildings, so read that section of your flood policy.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Read every denial reason</span><p>If one insurer denies part of the loss as the other peril, ask for the reason in writing and for any engineering report it relied on. That report often explains exactly which evidence the carrier weighed, and what evidence you may want to add.</p></div>

<h2>What to expect from two adjusters</h2>

<p>When wind and flood claims are open at the same time, you will usually deal with two different adjusters working for two different insurers, sometimes on different schedules. A few things help keep the two claims from working against each other:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Be consistent.</strong> Give both adjusters the same timeline and the same photos. Inconsistent descriptions of when or how water entered can be used to question either claim.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for each estimate in writing.</strong> Once both are issued, compare them room by room. Look for gaps, such as a wall that the windstorm estimate treats as flood and the flood estimate treats as wind.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the mitigation records shared.</strong> Drying logs, moisture readings, and tear-out photos are relevant to both claims.</li>
<li><strong>Track both sets of deadlines.</strong> A TWIA claim follows the deadlines in Texas Insurance Code Chapter 2210, Subchapter L-1, while a flood policy has its own proof-of-loss and claim requirements. Our <a href="/twia-claim-process-corpus-christi/">TWIA claim process guide</a> lists the TWIA side.</li>
</ul>

<p>Business owners face the same split, often with more at stake: inventory, equipment, and business interruption can all depend on which peril caused the damage. Clear documentation from the start matters even more there.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster fits in</h2>

<p>A licensed public adjuster represents you, not either insurer. On a mixed wind and water loss, that work may include documenting both perils, preparing separate estimates for the windstorm and flood claims, and pointing to the evidence that supports each. Corpus Christi Public Adjuster works with homeowners and businesses across Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities, including <a href="/service-areas/port-aransas-tx/">Port Aransas</a> and <a href="/service-areas/padre-island-tx/">Padre Island</a>. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Learn more about our <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> services.</p>

